  "slug": "depth-compartment-bb23",
  "summary": "New preprints adjust peak-gene and network inference for latent factors, perturbations and cross-modal discordance, and audit replicate-level noise floors.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is regulatory inference from multiomic and perturbational data, with explicit control of confounding and noise. CREMAP adjusts shared technical and biological latent variation before testing peak-gene links in single-cell multiome data. scOnion scores cell-level concordance between paired expression and chromatin accessibility rather than assuming the two modalities agree. Two works anchor inference in interventions. A TNBC framework combines CRISPRi Perturb-seq responses with TF-binding priors and nominates CTCF, MYC and AP-1 members as master regulators. ImmunoTwin maps CRISPR-screen regulators of T-cell and macrophage activation and reports a replicated coupling of T-cell exhaustion with macrophage M1 programs under checkpoint blockade. FiberTron predicts base-level chromatin, methylation and transcript patterns from Fiber-seq and nanopore RNA-seq of a single sample, which enables testing of variant effects. Two calibration studies set limits on interpretation. In drug-tolerant PC9 cells, replicate libraries define the noise floor for cell-state claims. Across 32,245 ATAC-seq experiments, text-metadata embeddings only weakly track accessibility similarity.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
